      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{9}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{x}^{-9/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{7}{x}^{-7/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{7}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{7}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{7}}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{7}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
