      "reasoning": "All 16 mathematical answers agree with their respective captured MA solutions. The source nevertheless contains authored errors: q-118618 drops the cube inside the square root on the left side of its final calculation, incorrectly displaying a different, divergent integral; its substitution, subsequent calculation, and answer 1 remain correct for the actual question. In q-31468 and q-167626, the original y-intercept is described as moving to a point with nonzero x-coordinate; those are translated points, not the new y-intercepts. Independently checking each question: q-65947 has arccos(1/2)=pi/3, arccos(sqrt(2)/2)=pi/4, and arccos(1)=0, so only II and III qualify. q-99055 has derivative -(x-1)(x-5)/(x^2-5)^2, giving critical numbers 1 and 5; ±sqrt(5) are outside the domain. q-114304 reduces to the integral of sin(x), giving 1-sqrt(2)/2. q-49275 reduces to sec^2(x)/8, giving tan(x)/8+C. q-49277 integrates to sec(x)+C because its derivative is sin(x)/cos^2(x). q-31468 is increasing with asymptote y=-4 and passes through (-5,-3), matching option 0 and the annotated source graph. q-167626 is increasing with asymptote y=-3 and passes through (-2,-2), matching option 0 and the annotated source graph. q-117888 has its zero at pi/21, so 3(pi/21)+C=0 modulo pi; the specified interval gives C=-pi/7. q-143669 decreases between asymptotes separated by pi/4, giving B=4; its midpoint value is D=-9/4, so B-2D=17/2. q-152777 integrates 2x^(-9/2) from 1 to infinity, giving 4/7. q-152762 integrates 2z^(-4/3) from 8 to infinity, giving 3. Substitution u=1+x^2 gives q-118619 as (1/2) integral from 1 to infinity of u^(-2), equal to 1/2, and q-118618 as (1/2) integral of u^(-3/2), equal to 1. q-20700 has antiderivative ln(ln(x)) and diverges to positive infinity. q-100643 has antiderivative -(2/3)sqrt(2-3t), and its lower-limit contribution diverges to positive infinity. q-49447 reduces to the integral of 10-4x^3, giving 10pi-pi^4. No submitted grades or earlier answers are provided; MA evidence here comes exclusively from each question's own worked solution.",
